<TopicPage slug="subjects/religious-studies/expert/religion-in-the-modern-world">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The modern world was supposed to make religion disappear. It didn't — it <strong>reorganised
    it</strong>: secular Europe, religious America, resurgent public religion, new movements, and
    fundamentalism as a distinctly modern style of protest. This lesson is the field's map of
    what actually happened — against the secularisation script everyone expected.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the secularisation thesis, fundamentalism, new movements, and public religion" widget="equationStep" config={modernCfg} />

  <h2>The scholarly arguments now</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Multiple modernities (Eisenstadt):</strong> modernity doesn't require Western secularism — Islamic, Hindu and Christian modernities are real alternatives, not failed copies of Europe.</li>
    <li><strong>Existential security theory (Norris & Ingleheart):</strong> religiosity tracks existential insecurity — rich secure societies secularise, poor precarious ones don't. The best-fitting data model, with the US as its big exception.</li>
    <li><strong>The "nones" rise:</strong> the fastest-growing religious identity in the West is "none" — but most nones are not atheists; they're detached, hybrid, spiritual-on-their-own-terms. Detraditionalisation, not extinction.</li>
    <li><strong>The scholar's posture:</strong> predicting religion's death has failed for 150 years. The modern discipline studies religion's transformations — migration, media, politics — with the humility the failed predictions taught.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Norris & Ingleheart, Sacred and Secular (2004); Eisenstadt, "Multiple Modernities" (2000); Pew Global Attitudes data; Casanova, Public Religions in the Modern World (1994).</p>
</TopicPage>
